<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Debo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Debo is a large lake in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-niger-delta">Inner Niger Delta</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, formed where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> widens between Mopti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. Its area varies greatly with the annual flood. The surrounding floodplain supports fishing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming and herding, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the region of Méma lay to its west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-debo.jpg" alt="Lake Debo" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jialiang Gao www.peace-on-earth.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niger_River_Center_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
